#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# interval

#% text
# `interval([a, b])` represents the closed interval from `a` to `b`.

#% text
# `interval([a, b], [c, d], ...)` represents a union of intervals.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Overlapping pieces are merged and the pieces sorted.

#% item
# Arithmetic follows interval arithmetic; dividing by an interval containing 0 splits the result.

#% item
# A function maps an interval to the span of its values at the ends and at the critical points inside (found from the derivative); exact ends give exact results where the function is monotone.

#% item
# Comparisons are decided when the intervals do not overlap.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
interval([1, 2]) + interval([3, 5])
#% output
#= interval([4, 7])

#% cell
interval([1, 2])*interval([-1, 3])
#% output
#= interval([-2, 6])

#% cell
1/interval([-1, 2])
#% output
#= interval([-inf, -1], [1/2, inf])

#% subsection
# Scope

#% cell
abs(interval([-3, 5]))
#% output
#= interval([0, 5])

#% cell
acos(interval([1/3, 1/2]))
#% output
#= interval([(1/3)*pi, acos(1/3)])

#% cell
gamma(interval([1.4, 1.5]))
#% output
#= interval([0.8856031944108886, 0.8872638175030754])

#% cell
interval([1, 2]) < 3
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Comparisons with intervals, around and quantities are decided where the sets do not overlap:

#% cell
interval([1, 2]) < around(3.0, 0.5)
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `around`, `centered_interval`
